[198.922624, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\n# Vulnerability Assessment & Entry Vector Report\n\n## Summary\nA rigorous forensic analysis of the software environment on `postgres-prod-01` (`/disk/var/lib/dpkg/status.summary:1`), the vulnerability feed (`/sas/vulns.json:1`), and authentication logs (`/disk/var/log/secure:155-214`) confirms that **this intrusion was NOT driven by a software vulnerability or CVE exploit**.\n\nSpecifically:\n1. **Vulnerability Feed**: The vulnerability scan feed `/sas/vulns.json` is explicitly empty (`[]`) (`/sas/vulns.json:1`). No known software vulnerabilities (CVEs) were identified or reported by automated vulnerability management tools.\n2. **Absence of Remote Exploit (e.g., CVE-2019-0708 / BlueKeep)**: Preliminary notes in draft remediation tickets (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:12`) erroneously suggested patching CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep). BlueKeep is a Microsoft Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) vulnerability specific to Windows operating systems. The target host is running CentOS Stream 9 / Linux (`/disk/etc/os-release:1`, `/disk/var/log/messages:1`), rendering CVE-2019-0708 completely irrelevant.\n3. **PostgreSQL Feature Abuse vs. Exploitation**: The entry and execution vector was the intentional abuse of built-in PostgreSQL functionality (`COPY ... TO PROGRAM`) facilitated by severe configuration errors (`trust` authentication in `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`) rather than a memory corruption or remote code execution flaw in PostgreSQL.\n\n## Analysis of Entry Vectors\n\n### Vector A: PostgreSQL Built-In Feature Abuse (`COPY ... TO PROGRAM`)\n- **Mechanism**: PostgreSQL includes a feature (`COPY table TO PROGRAM 'command'`) intended for database administrators to pipe data directly to or from external shell commands. When PostgreSQL is configured with superuser access (`postgres`) lacking password authentication, any network client can issue SQL commands that spawn arbitrary subshells with the privileges of the `postgres` daemon user.\n- **CVE Analysis**: `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` is by-design behavior for database superusers and does not have a assigned CVE. Privilege restriction is enforced through database authentication and superuser access controls.\n- **Evidence**: Superuser trust configuration in `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`), resulting in dropped payload `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`, cited in `/sas/alerts.json:1`).\n\n### Vector B: SSH Password Brute-Force\n- **Mechanism**: Attacker conducted an automated SSH password dictionary attack from `198.51.100.214` port 54883-61872 (`/disk/var/log/secure:155-213`) against accounts including `test`, `admin`, `oracle`, `postgres`, and `root`. The attack succeeded when the attacker guessed the password for `root` at 2026-08-23 00:55:55 UTC (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\n- **CVE Analysis**: Weak credential usage is an operational security failure, not a software vulnerability (CVE).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/log/secure:155`, `/disk/var/log/secure:214`.\n\n## Conclusion & Confidence Assessment\n- **CVE Exploitation**: None identified. Confidence: HIGH (`/sas/vulns.json:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n- **Primary Root Cause**: Administrative misconfiguration (PostgreSQL trust auth) and weak password policies (SSH root brute force).\nEOF\n"]
[198.922876, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md"]
