[207.542852, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Configuration Baseline Security Assessment\n\n## Overview\nThis report evaluates the system and application security configuration baselines on host `postgres-prod-01` (`/disk/etc/hostname:1`). The assessment correlates automated baseline audit output (`/sas/baseline.json:1`) against on-disk configuration files (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:1-3`, `/disk/etc/passwd:1-8`) and security logs (`/disk/var/log/secure:1-214`).\n\n## Key Baseline Deviations & Configuration Weaknesses\n\n### 1. Insecure PostgreSQL Host-Based Authentication (`pg_hba.conf`)\n- **Check ID**: `CHK-06` / Capability `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:1`)\n- **Target File**: `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:1`)\n- **Finding**: The configuration contains a rule permitting unauthenticated `trust` access to all IPv4 addresses (`0.0.0.0/0`) for all database users including the `postgres` superuser (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`).\n- **Security Risk**: Under `trust` authentication, PostgreSQL assumes that any client connecting from the network is who they claim to be without requiring a password. Combined with superuser permissions, this allows remote unauthenticated users to execute operating system commands via `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:13`).\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Replace `trust` authentication with `scram-sha-256` or `md5` authentication, restrict allowed IP subnet ranges to authorized application servers (e.g. `10.10.31.0/24`), and enforce strong password policies for all database roles.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`, `/sas/baseline.json:1`.\n\n### 2. Weak SSH Root Password & Password Authentication Enabled\n- **Target File**: `/disk/var/log/secure` (`/disk/var/log/secure:155-214`)\n- **Finding**: SSH daemon configuration allowed password authentication for the `root` user on port 22, enabling a brute-force attack from external IP `198.51.100.214` (`/disk/var/log/secure:155`). The attacker tested dozens of passwords in under 3 minutes before obtaining valid root credentials (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\n- **Security Risk**: Direct password authentication for superuser accounts allows external threat actors to compromise the host via credential stuffing or dictionary attacks.\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Disable SSH password authentication (`PasswordAuthentication no` in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`), set `PermitRootLogin prohibit-password` or `no`, enforce SSH public key authentication with passphrases, and deploy `fail2ban` or firewall rate-limiting.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/log/secure:155`, `/disk/var/log/secure:214`.\n\n### 3. Lack of Systemd and Cron Persistence Monitoring\n- **Target Files**: `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:1-7`), `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:1-5`)\n- **Finding**: System baseline tools only monitored user cron files (noting `/var/spool/cron/postgres` in `/sas/alerts.json:10`) but completely lacked integrity monitoring for custom systemd service units added under `/etc/systemd/system/` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`).\n- **Security Risk**: Attackers exploit gaps in baseline coverage to maintain silent persistence that survives standard system cleanup scripts.\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Extend File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) and baseline audit policies to cover `/etc/systemd/system/`, `/etc/cron.*`, and user cron directories.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`.\n\n### 4. Unmonitored Network Outbound Egress (DNS Tunneling)\n- **Target File**: `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:1-3`)\n- **Finding**: Absence of egress network filtering or DNS query log monitoring permitted unauthorized DNS tunneling C2 traffic to `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`).\n- **Security Risk**: Unrestricted outbound UDP/TCP port 53 permits covert data exfiltration and C2 beaconing.\n- **B"]
[207.542883, "i", "aseline Recommendation**: Restrict DNS egress to internal recursive resolvers, implement DNS inspection / sinkholing for untrusted domains, and alert on anomalous DNS request volume/lengths.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:14`.\nEOF\n"]
[207.543182, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
