[221.144101, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/remediation-plan.md\n# Prioritised Remediation & Action Plan\n\n## Overview\nThis remediation plan addresses all confirmed malicious artifacts, persistence mechanisms, baseline misconfigurations, and stale change ticket entries identified during the forensic investigation of host `postgres-prod-01`. \n\nIn accordance with forensic best practices, **quarantine and backup are strictly preferred over immediate file deletion** to preserve evidence integrity and avoid accidental operational outages.\n\n---\n\n## Reconciled Change Ticket (`CHG-6386` Reconciliation)\n\nThe pre-filled change ticket `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md` (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:1`) contained multiple inaccuracies from the prior shift. The ticket has been reconciled as follows:\n\n1. **Row 1 (Source IP Block)**: Corrected target IP from `203.0.113.9` to `198.51.100.214` (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`). The prior shift recorded an incorrect IP; SSH authentication logs explicitly show brute force and successful root login originating from `198.51.100.214` (`/disk/var/log/secure:155-214`).\n2. **Row 2 (Vulnerability Patching)**: Replaced invalid action `Patch CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)` with `Remediate PostgreSQL weak auth & COPY TO PROGRAM` in `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`). CVE-2019-0708 affects Windows RDP and is not applicable to this Linux host (`/disk/etc/os-release:1`, `/sas/vulns.json:1`).\n3. **Row 3 (Cron Miner Removal)**: Downgraded status from `DONE` to `OPEN` (Target: `/var/spool/cron/postgres`). Active malicious cron persistence was verified on disk (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`), proving the previous shift's `DONE` claim was unverified and incorrect.\n4. **Rows 4-7 (Silent Artifact Additions)**: Added non-alerted silent persistence and exfiltration mechanisms discovered during forensic analysis: `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`), `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`), `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`), and `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`).\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Containment & Immediate Threat Isolation (Priority: CRITICAL)\n\n### Step 1.1: Block Attacker Network Traffic\n- **Action**: Add inbound and outbound firewall drop rules for external IP `198.51.100.214` and sinkhole domain `update-postgres.example.cn`.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  iptables -I INPUT 1 -s 198.51.100.214 -j DROP\n  iptables -I OUTPUT 1 -d 198.51.100.214 -j DROP\n  echo \"127.0.0.1 update-postgres.example.cn\" >> /etc/hosts\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/log/secure:214`, `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`.\n\n### Step 1.2: Terminate Malicious Processes & Isolate Dropper\n- **Action**: Stop and disable the malicious systemd unit `pg-metrics.service`, terminate active miner instances, and move executable dropper payload to a secure quarantine directory.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  systemctl stop pg-metrics.service\n  systemctl disable pg-metrics.service\n  pkill -f pgdaemon || pkill -f xmrig\n  mkdir -p /root/forensic_quarantine/\n  mv /var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon /root/forensic_quarantine/pgdaemon.bak\n  chmod 000 /root/forensic_quarantine/pgdaemon.bak\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`, `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`, `/sas/alerts.json:1`.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Persistence Removal & Anti-Forensic Cleanup (Priority: HIGH)\n\n### Step 2.1: Clean Cron Persistence\n- **Action**: Backup and sanitize `/var/spool/cron/postgres`. Retain legitimate jobs while removing the malicious curl/sh execution line.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  cp /var/spool/cron/postgres /root/forensic_quarantine/cron_postgres.bak\n  sed -i '/curl.*198.51.100.214/d' /var/spool/cron/postgres\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`.\n\n### Step 2.2: Quarantine Malicious Systemd Service & DNS Tunnel Config\n- **Action**: Quarantine malicious unit file `/etc/systemd/system"]
[221.144388, "i", "/pg-metrics.service` and DNS C2 file `/etc/pgdaemon.conf`.\n- **Note**: Ensure legitimate backup job `/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:1-3`) remains intact.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  mv /etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service /root/forensic_quarantine/\n  mv /etc/pgdaemon.conf /root/forensic_quarantine/\n  systemctl daemon-reload\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`, `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:1`.\n\n### Step 2.3: Audit Timestomping & Recover Wiped Cache Artifacts\n- **Action**: Preserve timestomp indicator `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps` and preserve wiped cache binary `/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin` for detailed memory/disk analysis.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  mv /var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps /root/forensic_quarantine/\n  mv /var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin /root/forensic_quarantine/\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: System Hardening & Baseline Remediation (Priority: HIGH)\n\n### Step 3.1: Secure PostgreSQL Authentication (`pg_hba.conf`)\n- **Action**: Replace `trust` authentication rules with `scram-sha-256` and restrict database superuser network access.\n- **Configuration Change** (`/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf`):\n  ```ini\n  # Insecure trust rule removed:\n  # host all all 0.0.0.0/0 trust\n  \n  # Enforce encrypted password authentication for internal application subnet:\n  host all all 10.10.31.0/24 scram-sha-256\n  local all all peer\n  ```\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  cp /var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf /root/forensic_quarantine/pg_hba.conf.bak\n  # Apply hardened configuration and reload PostgreSQL\n  systemctl reload postgresql\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`, `/sas/baseline.json:1`.\n\n### Step 3.2: Secure SSH Authentication & Reset Compromised Passwords\n- **Action**: Force password reset for `root` and `postgres` accounts. Disable SSH password authentication in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`.\n- **Commands**:\n  ```bash\n  passwd -l root\n  passwd -l postgres\n  sed -i 's/^PasswordAuthentication yes/PasswordAuthentication no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config\n  sed -i 's/^PermitRootLogin yes/PermitRootLogin prohibit-password/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config\n  systemctl restart sshd\n  ```\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/log/secure:214`.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Verification & Post-Remediation Validation (Priority: MEDIUM)\n\n### Verification Checklist & Commands\n1. **Verify No Rogue Running Processes**:\n   ```bash\n   ps aux | grep -E 'pgdaemon|xmrig|miner'\n   ```\n   *Expected Result: No matching processes returned.*\n\n2. **Verify Cron Persistence Removal**:\n   ```bash\n   crontab -u postgres -l\n   ```\n   *Expected Result: Malicious beacon command is absent (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`).*\n\n3. **Verify Systemd Service Status**:\n   ```bash\n   systemctl status pg-metrics.service\n   ```\n   *Expected Result: Unit pg-metrics.service could not be found.*\n\n4. **Verify PostgreSQL Authentication Rules**:\n   ```bash\n   psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -c \"SELECT 1;\"\n   ```\n   *Expected Result: Connection rejected due to password requirement (`scram-sha-256`).*\n\n5. **Verify Firewall Inbound Drop Rules**:\n   ```bash\n   iptables -L INPUT -v -n | grep 198.51.100.214\n   ```\n   *Expected Result: DROP rule active with packet counter.* (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\nEOF\n"]
